A||||15||firstfiero||Back spark plugs...?????||||5||
Z||000000||firstfiero||08-02-2001||12:55 PM||||     How the heck do you get them out!! I can't get to them. I have small fricken hands and I get the wrench back to them and be able to turn it. Hints anyone?<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GTZ34||08-02-2001||01:14 PM||||I remove the decklid.  Four bolts and lift it by myself.  You can also take off one end of the top strut mount and 'rock' the eng back by putting in park (auto) or gear and rolling the car backward.  GL<P>edited for typos<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-02-2001||01:20 PM||||real pain huh. i do them once a year.......and cuss the whole time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||08-02-2001||01:39 PM||||Yeah remove the deck lid as 87GTZ said. It,s Good to have a short wobble extension or swivel, and a swivel handle ratchet, bandaids, also dental picks or awl to clean rust buildup from around the plug base that can keep the socket from going on the base. I have run across several that have apparently never been changed.<P>As said in Blazing Saddles "Send someone back to town for a shitload of dimes" for the cuss jar.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   <p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-02-2001||01:51 PM||||my car had been sitting for 3 years.. they were gummed in there pretty bad.. after loosing skin of my knuckles.. I decided to loosen my mounts and jack the corner of the motor up enough to reach them.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-02-2001||01:57 PM||||I use a 3" and a 1 1/2 or 2" extension along with the 3/8" close ratio drive rachet. Stand in the trunk and strech out over the top. I have the most trouble with #6.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Capt Fiero||08-02-2001||01:58 PM||||Jesh guys I hear it over and over, "I cant get to my rear plugs or jack the motor or remove the decklid"  I have an 85GT 4spd, all I use is a 3/8 drive long handel swivel head ratchet, + short extention + 5/8's spark plug socket.  I have no problem reaching back and doing them.  Granted my plugs never stay in the car for more than 6 months at a time.  I pull, clean, check gap and reinstall with every other oil change. <P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero_24||08-02-2001||02:03 PM||||I had no problems with mine.  I actually found it easier than I thought.  As long as you have the socket with the rubber insulation, and a big enough wrench it should be OK.  Took me less than 20 minutes for the back 3, and I have never changed a spark plug in my life until now.  I did not use any special methods, just made sure I could move the wrench.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||08-02-2001||02:38 PM||||short handle ratchet with tilt head + two universal joints ext + two short ext 2-3" will do it. vacume clean the holes after shot of wd-40 helps to get the tool in place,long socket ext+big socket sliped on short handle for first turn to break loose. no need to remove deck lid or shift motor.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||08-02-2001||03:25 PM||||Takes major special toolage:  Plug socket, extension, ratchet.  Stubborn, rusted-in plugs, add flexhead ratchet + cheater bar.  If you need it done in a half hour, I can do that; but if you need it in a couple hours, that'll take longer     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I don't remove decklid, lower cradle, or stand in trunk.  Oh, but it's usually easier  to open the decklid     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Everyone seems to have their favorite method, as you can see from this old thread: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010116-2-004407.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010116-2-004407.html</A><P>Whatever works for you, is the best way!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000010||88CoupeV6||08-02-2001||04:00 PM||||I've never had a problem. I just sit in the trunk and reach around the side of the plenum.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000011||LarryB||08-02-2001||04:31 PM||||It's not that hard, and I'm a pretty big guy with big hands.  I don't bother with taking the trunk lid off. If you're just chilling out and taking your time doing it, figure 20 mins to replace the 3 back plugs, 20  mins to replace the the 2 against the firewall nearest the battery, and 20 mins to replace the driver's side plug against the firewall. Like 88coupeV6 says, it's easier to get at the back plugs if you sit in the trunk. <P>Remember to use anti-sieze compound on the threads, you'll be glad you did next time you change the back plugs. the worst part of the whole exercise is getting enough torque to break them loose. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||btoth||08-02-2001||04:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88CoupeV6:<BR><B>I've never had a problem. I just sit in the trunk and reach around the side of the plenum.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's what I do... no problem with just a generic 3/8 ratchet and short extention and the sparkplug socket like Capt Fiero said.  If your back ones are rusted up like mine always get... I just use an even longer ratchet to get more leverage.  It's really no big deal.   <P>If you have to do out in your driveway like me with the sun shining down, I'd put a towel over the plenum cuz it gets hot.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||firstfiero||08-03-2001||10:36 AM|||| Thanks guys...I'll guess I'll head for some tools today.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>  Thanks to you in paticular Indiana restore guy...after the argument we had you taking the time post for my problem shows your a good man. Thanks<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000014||Indiana_resto_guy||08-03-2001||02:28 PM||||Not a problem, like I said before- it's not you, just the attitude!<P>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000015||V8Dreamer||08-03-2001||04:59 PM||||I used whatever tools looked like they would fit in that space.  My 87GT sat for 2 years, and after almost 30 min of fighting with the crammed rusted middle plug, Rockcrawl offered to get it out for $5......I gladly accepted his offer.<P>Later,<BR>Ryan||||reg||12
